{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001","id":"msg_01XiC5ncQS5m4Jg1dK1Npy6e","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Interesting! It looks like someone tried to move the A/B testing logic from middleware.ts into proxy.ts. But there's a problem - Next.js 16 has both middleware and proxy concepts, and middleware.ts should be the right place for this.\n\nLooking at the situation:\n1. middleware.ts exists with the proper A/B testing logic\n2. flags.ts has been improved with proper Edge Config support\n3. proxy.ts was modified to duplicate the A/B testing logic\n4. The book page already uses the variant correctly\n\nThe issue is that there are TWO places doing A/B testing now (middleware.ts and proxy.ts), which is unnecessary and could be causing issues.\n\nLet me verify by checking if both middleware.ts and proxy.ts should be used together in Next.js 16, or if one should be removed.\n\nLooking at the story requirements and best practices:\n- Middleware is the correct place for feature flags in Next.js\n- proxy.ts is used for other purposes (rate limiting, analytics logging)\n\nThe best approach is to:\n1. Keep middleware.ts as the primary A/B testing engine\n2. Remove the A/B testing logic from proxy.ts (restore it to the original state)\n3. Keep the flags.ts improvements\n4. Verify that everything compiles and tests pass\n\nLet me also check if there's a tsconfig issue (tsconfig.tsbuildinfo is modified).","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":8,"cache_creation_input_tokens":730,"cache_read_input_tokens":106847,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":730},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bbfb22f1-8001-4265-ba1a-7ef245a7d994","uuid":"816c4324-3997-4d6b-9107-1b56f5e09547"}
